## Web UI

Server-rendered HTML (this is **not** a JSON API). Handlers are in `internal/adapters/handlers/{{.Platform}}`; routes live under `/users`:

| Method | Path | Renders |
|---|---|---|
| `GET` | `/users` | list page |
| `GET` | `/users/:id` | detail page |
| `POST` | `/users` | create (form submit) |
| `PUT` | `/users/:id` | update |
| `DELETE` | `/users/:id` | delete |

The list page is **forward-only paginated**: when more rows remain it renders a `Next →` link carrying an opaque `?cursor=…` (default page size `20`). This is a deliberately minimal browse UX — there is no "Previous" (backward cursors are out of scope). A hand-edited, malformed `?cursor` surfaces as a `400`; the generated `Next` links always carry a valid cursor.

Static assets are served from `/assets/*`. HTML templates and CSS live in `internal/web` and are **embedded into the binary** via `//go:embed`, so the compiled binary is self-contained (no runtime file dependencies). The server listens on `:8080` by default (see `PORT`).
{{- end}}
{{- if $hasHTTP}}

## Health & readiness

Two orchestration endpoints are mounted at the transport layer (in `cmd/server`), so they work regardless of the HTTP framework:

| Method | Path | Purpose | Success |
|---|---|---|---|
| `GET` | `/healthz` | liveness — the process is running | `200 {"status":"ok"}` |
| `GET` | `/readyz` | readiness — ready to accept traffic | `200 {"status":"ready"}` |

Wire `/healthz` to a liveness probe and `/readyz` to a readiness probe (e.g. Kubernetes). The handlers live in `internal/adapters/handlers/health`; extend `Ready` to check critical dependencies (database, broker) and return `503` when any is unavailable, so the readiness probe can gate traffic.
{{- if .OpenAPI}}

## API documentation (OpenAPI / Swagger)

The API is documented by an OpenAPI 3.0.3 spec served by the app, with interactive Swagger UI:

| Method | Path | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `GET` | `/openapi.yaml` | the raw OpenAPI document (`application/yaml`) |
| `GET` | `/swagger/` | interactive Swagger UI ("try it out") |

Both are mounted at the transport layer in `cmd/server` (so they work for every framework) and are **self-contained**: the spec is embedded via `//go:embed` and the Swagger UI assets are compiled into the binary by `swaggest/swgui` — no CDN, nothing to deploy alongside. The serving handlers are `api.SpecHandler` / `api.SwaggerUIHandler` in `internal/adapters/api`.{{if .Auth}} The spec declares a `bearerAuth` security scheme; use `POST /login` to get a token, then paste it into Swagger UI's **Authorize** dialog.{{end}}

**The spec is templated, not scraped** — it lives in `internal/adapters/api/openapi.yaml` and was written to match the generated handlers. It is **not** auto-derived from them, so **when you add or change a route, update `openapi.yaml` too** (same discipline as this file and the README). If you'd rather have the spec regenerated from code, swap to annotation-driven generation with [swaggo/swag](https://github.com/swaggo/swag) (`// @Summary`/`// @Router` comments + a `swag init` build step) — more machinery, but it re-derives the doc from annotated handlers.
{{- end}}

## Middleware

Requests to the application handler pass through a framework-agnostic middleware chain composed in `cmd/server` (package `internal/adapters/handlers/middleware`): **recover** (a panic becomes a logged `500`, not a crash), **request logging** (method, path, status, duration), **CORS** (permissive by default — tighten `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` before production), and a **per-request timeout** (bounds the request `context` so a slow datastore call can't hang forever). The health endpoints are mounted outside the chain, so probes stay cheap and unlogged.
{{- end}}
{{- if $hasObservability}}

## Observability (OpenTelemetry)

This project exports **traces and metrics** over OTLP/gRPC to a collector. The setup lives in `internal/adapters/observability` — the only package that imports the OTel SDK. The core stays free of it (a span rides inside `context.Context`), and `make arch` enforces that.

- **Instrumented:** {{if $hasHTTP}}inbound HTTP requests (one span each, via `otelhttp` at the transport wrap in `cmd/server`){{else}}each consumed message (a span in `cmd/main`){{end}}, plus **Go runtime metrics** (GC, goroutines, heap) and W3C trace-context propagation.
- **Config is all `OTEL_*` env vars in `.env`** — endpoint, `OTEL_SERVICE_NAME`, resource attributes, and the trace sampler (`OTEL_TRACES_SAMPLER`/`_ARG`) are read natively by the SDK; no code override. Lower the sampler ratio in production and/or tail-sample in the collector.
- **Local pipeline:** `make docker-up` starts an `otel-collector` (config `configs/otel-collector.yaml`) that logs received telemetry. Add a real backend (Jaeger, Tempo, Prometheus, a vendor) by extending that config — no app change.
- **Fail-open:** if the collector is unreachable the app still starts and {{if $hasHTTP}}serves{{else}}runs{{end}}; export errors are silenced (`otel.SetErrorHandler`) so they don't flood logs. Buffered telemetry flushes on shutdown{{if $hasHTTP}}, after the HTTP server drains{{else}}, after the consumer goroutine is joined{{end}}.
- **Adding instrumentation:** do it in **adapters** or `cmd`, never the core. Grab a tracer with `otel.Tracer("name")` and start a span from the request/message `context.Context`. A business-logic span belongs behind a port, not a direct SDK import in `services`.
{{- if $hasHTTP}}
- **Route labels:** the HTTP wrap is framework-agnostic, so spans/metrics are **not** split per route (every endpoint reports route `/`). For per-endpoint RED metrics, add framework middleware (`otelecho`/`otelgin`/…).
{{- end}}
{{- end}}
{{- if $hasAuth}}

## Authentication (JWT)

Every application route requires a bearer token; a demo `POST /login` issues one. The logic lives in `internal/adapters/handlers/auth` (the only package that imports the JWT library — the core stays free of it; the authenticated subject travels in `context.Context`, readable via `auth.SubjectFrom(ctx)`).

- **Flow:** `POST /login` with `{"username","password"}` (the demo `AUTH_USERNAME`/`AUTH_PASSWORD` from `.env`) returns `{"token":"…"}`; send it as `Authorization: Bearer <token>` on every other route. Missing/invalid/expired → `401`.
- **Config** is `JWT_*`/`AUTH_*` in `.env`, read in `cmd/server`. **HS256** with an env secret; the algorithm is **pinned** (`WithValidMethods`) so `alg:none` and RS256→HS256 confusion are rejected.
- **All-or-nothing:** the auth middleware wraps the whole framework handler, so **every** app route is protected; the only public endpoints are `/healthz`, `/readyz`, and `/login`. For mixed public/protected app routes, add per-framework middleware (echo groups, chi sub-routers, …).
{{- if $isGraphql}}
- **GraphQL note:** `/graphql` is fully behind the bearer token — send it in `Authorization`. Introspection and the Playground therefore require a token too (disabling public introspection is a normal prod-hardening step); expose public queries via per-framework middleware if you need them.
{{- end}}
- **Productionize before shipping:** the loud `JWT_SECRET` placeholder must become a strong (≥32-byte) secret from a secret store, rotated; and the demo env-credential check in `LoginHandler` must be replaced with a **real user store + password hash** (bcrypt/argon2). Serve over TLS only (a bearer token is a password). Not yet included: refresh tokens, revocation, and role/scope authorization.
{{- end}}
{{- if $hasBroker}}

## Broker workflow
{{- if $isWorker}}

This worker has **no HTTP server**. On startup, `cmd/main.go`:

1. connects to **{{.Broker}}** (adapter: `internal/adapters/brokers/{{.Broker}}`);
2. publishes one sample `Message{Data: "Hello World"}` (JSON) to the `testing` {{if eq .Broker "kafka"}}topic{{else if eq .Broker "rabbitmq"}}queue{{else}}channel{{end}};
3. runs a consume loop that {{if $hasRealDB}}persists every message through the user service (only failures are logged){{else}}logs every message as `message received`{{end}};
4. shuts down gracefully on `SIGINT`/`SIGTERM` (via `signal.NotifyContext`).
{{- else}}

This {{if $isWeb}}web app{{else}}API{{end}} adds event messaging **alongside** its HTTP server (one binary):

1. **Producer** — the user service holds an `EventPublisher` port (wired with `WithPublisher`), so a write (e.g. creating a user) publishes an event to **{{.Broker}}** (adapter: `internal/adapters/brokers/{{.Broker}}`). The core stays broker-agnostic — it depends on the port, not the client.
2. **Consumer** — a consume loop runs in a goroutine next to the HTTP server, logging every message as `event received`, and is joined on shutdown (a `sync.WaitGroup`, as in the `worker` type) so in-flight messages finish.
3. Both share the process's signal context, so `SIGINT`/`SIGTERM` drains the HTTP server and stops the consumer together.
{{- end}}

{{if eq .Broker "redis"}}The consumer subscribes at construction, so a message published on the `testing` channel while it is running is delivered. Redis pub/sub drops messages sent while no subscriber is connected — publish from another client (e.g. `redis-cli PUBLISH testing '...'`) to feed it.{{else if eq .Broker "kafka"}}Kafka retains the topic log, so messages are consumed even if produced before the consumer starts. Produce more messages to the `testing` topic from any Kafka client.{{else}}RabbitMQ queues messages, so they are consumed even if produced before the consumer starts. Publish more messages to the `testing` queue from any AMQP client.{{end}}
{{- end}}

